Cosmopolitan)\nciteturn21search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 23.8 years ago; Crawled: last week; # Cosmopolitan: A Bartender's Life ... great book if you want to read about the life of a bartender - a classy one - in a NY bar (or many).\n\n# Cosmopolitan: A Bartender's Life\n\n### \n\nToby Cecchini\n\n3.70 196 ratings27 reviews\n\nWant to Read\n\nRate this book\n\nCosmopolitan is a memoir of the bartending life structured as a day in the life of Passerby, the bar owned and run by Toby Cecchini. It is, as well, a rich study of human nature—of the outlandish behavior of the human animal under the influence of alcohol, of lust, and of the sheer desire to bust loose and party. As the typical day progresses, Cecchini muses over a life spent in the service industry and the fascinating particulars of his chosen profession. He is by turns witty, acute, mordant, and lyrical in dealing with the realities of his profession, shedding plenty of light on the hidden corners of what people do when they go out at night.\n\nGenresMemoirNonfictionCocktailsFoodBiography\n\n256 pages, Paperback\n\nFirst published January 1, 2003\n\nBook details & editions\n\n### About the author\n\n#### Toby Cecchini\n\n4 books 1 follower\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n→ The Best Cosmopolitan Cocktail History | MissCharming.com (https://www.misscharming.com/cosmopolitancocktailhistory)\nciteturn21search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Toby Cecchini's account, as published in his 2003 memoir Cosmopolitan: A Bartender's Life, states that in 1987, a co-worker (\"Mesa,\" i.e., Melissa Huffsmith) showed him a cocktail she had learned from a friend visiting from San Francisco, made with grenadine and Rose's lime juice. ... In addition, Bob Sennett’s Complete World Bartender Guide (1993) is the second known book I have found to mention the Cosmopolitan, and its recipe produces a red cocktail.\n \n\nTHE COSMOPOLITAN IN BOOKS\n\n1993\n\nApril 23, The Complete Book of Mixed Drinks by Anthony Dias Blue.\n\nThis is the first known book to mention the Cosmopolitan. The recipe makes a pink cocktail. The author mentions the Cosmopolitan at Julie's Supper Club in San Francisco. Thank you, Marco Dionysos. \n\n​\n\nNovember 1, Complete World Bartender Guide by Bob Sennett.\n\nThis is the second known book to mention the Cosmopolitan. The recipe makes a red cocktail. The photo includes a matchbook from The Odeon, next to a red Cosmopolitan.\n\nOctober 1, The New York Bartender’s Guide by Sally Ann Berk.\n\nOctober 2, The New York Times mentions the Rainbow Room's (Dale DeGroff) Cosmopolitan. After Cheryl Cook's death in 2026, New York Times reporter Alex Traub contacted me about my Cosmopolitan research, calling the history “extraordinary” and “outstanding.” I shared my research on Cheryl, creator of the Miami Beach Cosmopolitan, and in return he gifted me a copy of the first Cosmopolitan article published in The New York Times. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChangeThis\nSave to disk [help]\nHide/Show menus\nY\n2 (https://www.turtletrader.com/pdfs/100Ways.pdf)\nciteturn21search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Call this Success Tip #37, and NYResolution2005 #1. ... derful little manuscript-book I received, BEYOND CODE, by Rajesh Setty.\nChangeThis\n100 WAYS TO SUCCEED #37: \nTO LIVE IS THE RAREST THING IN THE WORLD\n“Nobody can prevent you from choosing to be exceptional.”\n—Mark Sanborn, The Fred Factor\nCall this Success Tip #37, and NYResolution2005 #1.\nOkay?\n(Hint: I have tried using this as a Right Breathing Mantra: NOBODY CAN PREVENT ME FROM \nBEING EXCEPTIONAL. It works wonderfully.*) (*And is still worth repeating at age 62.)\n100 WAYS TO SUCCEED #38: \nRE-VISIT/RE-IMAGINE YOUR VA PROPOSITION.\nDue date: 15 January.\nHyundai. Home Depot. BRANDsense (BRANDsense: Build Powerful Brands through Touch, \nTaste, Smell, Sight, and Sound, by renowned futurist Mar